====================================================================================================================
ITEM 37 -- is the LMC bound to a Milky Way made only of baryons?
====================================================================================================================
  measured: R_GC = 50.1 kpc, v_rad = +64, v_tan = 314, v_tot = 321 +- 24 km/s
  the framework's Milky Way: M_b = 6.00e+10 Msun of baryons and nothing else; LambdaCDM's: M_200 ~ 1e12 with M_b the same
  MW MOND radius r_M = sqrt(G M_b/a_0) = 9.5 (canonical) / 8.6 (alt) kpc -- the LMC at 50 kpc sits 5.3 r_M out, deep in the modified regime
  canonical  M31's MONDian pull on the MW at 780 kpc = 0.0171 a_0 -> Newtonian-equivalent e_N = 2.89e-04
  alt        M31's MONDian pull on the MW at 780 kpc = 0.0156 a_0 -> Newtonian-equivalent e_N = 2.40e-04
  the large-scale-structure field is independently put at 0.01-0.03 a_0 (MONDian), i.e. e_N ~ 1e-4 to 1e-3 Newtonian;
  the scan below therefore covers e_N = 1e-4 ... 3e-2, three decades, and reports where the verdict changes.

        e_N  r_ext[kpc] v_esc(canonical)       v_esc(alt)   verdict (canonical / alt)
    1.0e-04       860.4            451.1            466.3   BOUND  / BOUND 
    3.0e-04       496.7            416.7            429.7   BOUND  / BOUND 
    1.0e-03       272.1            375.5            385.8   BOUND  / BOUND 
    3.0e-03       157.1            334.3            341.9   BOUND  / BOUND 
    6.0e-03       111.1            306.4            312.2   UNBOUND / UNBOUND
    1.0e-02        86.0            285.1            289.6   UNBOUND / UNBOUND
    3.0e-02        49.7            238.4            240.5   UNBOUND / UNBOUND

  canonical  the LMC is bound iff the external field on the MW is WEAKER than e_N = 4.19e-03 (Newtonian) = 0.0669 a_0 (MONDian)
  alt        the LMC is bound iff the external field on the MW is WEAKER than e_N = 4.90e-03 (Newtonian) = 0.0725 a_0 (MONDian)
  the independent estimate of that field is 0.01-0.03 a_0 MONDian (M31 alone gives 0.017; the LSS the rest),
  i.e. e_N = 9.90e-05 to 8.74e-04 Newtonian -- BELOW the threshold by a factor 2.2 at the strong end.  So the framework's answer is: BOUND.
  [PASS] 37 the framework makes a DEFINITE prediction here and it is not a fitted one: with the Milky Way's baryons alone, no halo, and the external field pinned independently at 0.01-0.03 a_0, the escape speed at the LMC's position is 375-451 km/s (canonical) and the LMC is BOUND.  Newtonian baryons without the kernel give 102 km/s and would have it long gone   (v_esc = 380-451 (canonical) / 391-467 (alt) km/s across the external-field range, against a measured v_LMC = 321 +- 24)
  [PASS] 37b ...and the margin is thin enough to be a live falsifier rather than a comfortable pass: at the strongest plausible external field the LMC is bound by only 2.5 sigma of its own measured speed, and the verdict turns over at e_N = 4.2e-3 (0.067 a_0 MONDian), a factor 2.2 above the highest independent estimate.  A 15% larger LMC speed, or an external field twice the LSS estimate, breaks it   (least-bound case v_esc = 380 vs 321 +- 24 -> margin 2.5 sigma; turnover at e_N = 4.19e-03 = 0.067 a_0 MONDian vs the estimated 0.01-0.03)
  [PASS] 37c READ THE OTHER WAY: because the LMC is observed to be there at all, its proper motion places an UPPER BOUND on the external field acting on the Milky Way -- e_N < 4.2e-3, i.e. g_ext < 0.067 a_0 -- which is a measurement of a quantity the framework otherwise has to import from large-scale structure.  Nothing in LambdaCDM has an analogue of it   (g_ext(MW) < 0.067 a_0 (canonical) / 0.072 a_0 (alt), at the measured v_LMC; the bound weakens to 0.049 a_0 if the LMC is 1 sigma faster)

  LambdaCDM (NFW M200 = 1e12, c = 10, r200 = 212 kpc, plus the same baryons): v_esc(50 kpc) = 388 km/s -> BOUND with margin
  Newtonian baryons only, no halo and no kernel (the mutation floor): v_esc = 102 km/s -> UNBOUND by a factor 3.2
  [PASS] M37 mutation: switching the kernel off (nu = 1, the same baryons) must destroy the result -- the escape speed collapses to a third of the measured LMC speed, so the pass above is the kernel's doing and not an accident of the mass   (nu = 1 gives v_esc = 102 km/s vs the kernel's 380-451 and the measured 321)
  [PASS] M37b mutation: a_0 -> a_0/100 must also destroy it   (a_0/100 gives v_esc = 188 km/s)

  M_b(MW) = 5.0e+10 Msun at e_N = 3e-4: v_esc = 392 (canonical) / 404 (alt) km/s
  M_b(MW) = 6.0e+10 Msun at e_N = 3e-4: v_esc = 417 (canonical) / 430 (alt) km/s
  M_b(MW) = 7.0e+10 Msun at e_N = 3e-4: v_esc = 439 (canonical) / 453 (alt) km/s
  v_esc goes as M_b^(1/4) x sqrt(log), so a 17% mass error moves it by 4% -- the external field, not the baryon budget, is what decides.

====================================================================================================================
the orbit: first passage or not, integrated backwards
====================================================================================================================
  canonical  e_N = 9.9e-05 (0.010 a_0): backwards 13 Gyr the LMC reaches     294 kpc, 3 pericentric passage(s), radial period 4.8 Gyr
  canonical  e_N = 8.7e-04 (0.030 a_0): backwards 13 Gyr the LMC reaches     366 kpc, 2 pericentric passage(s), radial period 6.8 Gyr
  canonical  e_N = 1.0e-02 (0.105 a_0): backwards 13 Gyr the LMC reaches    2240 kpc, 1 pericentric passage(s), radial period > 13 Gyr (does not return -- first infall)
  alt        e_N = 9.9e-05 (0.010 a_0): backwards 13 Gyr the LMC reaches     246 kpc, 4 pericentric passage(s), radial period 3.9 Gyr
  alt        e_N = 8.7e-04 (0.030 a_0): backwards 13 Gyr the LMC reaches     297 kpc, 3 pericentric passage(s), radial period 5.3 Gyr
  alt        e_N = 1.0e-02 (0.105 a_0): backwards 13 Gyr the LMC reaches    2143 kpc, 1 pericentric passage(s), radial period > 13 Gyr (does not return -- first infall)
  LambdaCDM NFW (M200 = 1e12, c = 10): backwards 13 Gyr the LMC reaches     339 kpc, 2 pericentric passage(s), radial period 6.7 Gyr
  [PASS] 37d the ORBIT does not discriminate either: at the estimated external field the framework's LMC is on a bound orbit with an apocentre of a few hundred kpc and several pericentric passages in a Hubble time, and a 1e12 Msun NFW halo gives the same picture to within 0.2 dex.  Boundness and orbit shape are NOT where these two theories differ about the LMC   (canonical apocentre 366 kpc (2 passages) vs NFW 339 kpc (2 passages); 0.03 dex apart)
  a real difference does hide here and it is worth naming: MOND has NO dynamical friction against a halo that does not
  exist, so a bound MOND LMC has made these passages without sinking, and the Magellanic Stream has to be made on a
  multiple-passage orbit rather than on first infall.  Testing that needs a Stream model, not an energy budget.

  THE LIABILITY THAT THIS ITEM ACTUALLY EXPOSES, stated plainly:
    LambdaCDM needs an LMC of ~1.5e+11 Msun to make the Magellanic Stream, the wake in the stellar halo and the
    reflex motion of the outer disc.  The framework's LMC is 3.2e+09 Msun of baryons -- a factor 47 lighter --
    and MOND has no dynamical friction against a halo that does not exist, so the LMC cannot have sunk.  The framework must
    make the Stream and the wake with 3e9 Msun and the kernel's own boost; whether it can is NOT tested here and is the
    place this item should go next.  Recording it as an OPEN liability, not as a null.
    order of magnitude: at 50 kpc the MW's field is 0.19 a_0, so the LMC's internal dynamics are EFE-suppressed and its effective pull on the halo is nearer G x 3.2e+09 than G x 1.5e+11.
  [PASS] 37e the discriminator is NOT boundness (both theories can be made to bind or not) but the LMC's own MASS: the framework gives the LMC 3.2e9 Msun of baryons where LambdaCDM's Stream-and-wake modelling needs ~1.5e11 -- a factor 47 that no external field can absorb.  Recorded as the open half of item 37   (factor 47 between the two LMC masses; the escape-speed test above resolves nothing next to it)

